import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={schrodingerCat:{theory:{concept:"[[Schr\xF6dinger's cat is a thought experiment in which the fate of a cat in a closed box depends on whether one radioactive atom has decayed; it was invented to show how strange it would be if quantum superposition applied to everyday things.]] Erwin Schr\xF6dinger described it in 1935. Quantum mechanics predicts only the chance that the atom decays, never the moment. Taken literally, it would say that until someone opens the box the cat is in a superposition of both states \u2014 which nobody has ever seen. In this version the device releases a harmless sleeping gas instead of Schr\xF6dinger's poison, so the cat is either asleep or awake."},section:{setup:{title:"The box",text:"The closed box holds a cat, one radioactive atom, a Geiger counter and a flask of sleeping gas. [[If the atom decays, the counter clicks, a valve opens and the cat falls asleep; if it does not decay, the cat stays awake.]] Nobody can look inside until the box is opened. Schr\xF6dinger's original device released poison; the gas changes nothing in the physics."},chance:{title:"Only chances, never the moment",text:"Nobody can predict when one particular atom will decay \u2014 not even in principle. Quantum mechanics gives only a probability: [[after each half-life T\xBD the chance that the atom is still undecayed halves: P_awake = (1/2)^(t/T\xBD).]] After one half-life the cat is asleep with a chance of 50 %, after two half-lives 75 %, after three 87.5 %. Another experiment number gives another random outcome, just as another box would."},superposition:{title:"Asleep and awake at once?",text:"Before anyone measures it, quantum mechanics describes the atom by a superposition: |\u03C8\u27E9 = \u221AP_awake\xB7|\u03C8\u2080\u27E9 + \u221AP_asleep\xB7|\u03C8\u2081\u27E9, where \u03C8\u2080 is the undecayed atom and \u03C8\u2081 the decayed one. The numbers in front are amplitudes, and [[the probability of a result is the square of its amplitude (the Born rule).]] When the box is opened exactly one result is found \u2014 never a half-asleep cat \u2014 and the amplitudes only tell how often each result comes up. While the box is closed, the bars show these probabilities growing."},absurd:{title:"Schr\xF6dinger's point",text:"If you applied the atom's rules to the counter, the gas and the cat as well, and forgot about their surroundings, the whole box would be in a superposition until it is opened: a cat asleep and awake at the same time. Schr\xF6dinger meant this as a criticism: [[nobody has ever seen a cat in two states at once, so something must happen between the atom and the cat.]] The question of where the quantum rules stop and the ordinary, definite world begins is called the measurement problem."},decoherence:{title:"Why we never see such a cat",text:"Today the main part of the answer is decoherence. The counter and the cat are made of countless atoms that keep bumping into air molecules, light and each other. [[Contact with the surroundings destroys a superposition of large objects almost at once]] \u2014 for a cat in far less than 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 s. In practice the counter clicks or does not, and the cat is asleep or awake; we just do not know which until we look. \u201CObserving\u201D means any such interaction, not a person looking. Decoherence explains why we never see both at once. Why one particular result appears is still discussed by physicists (the interpretations of quantum mechanics), but all of them predict the same chances that the simulation shows."},many:{title:"Many boxes",text:"One box gives one random answer. With N identical boxes the share of sleeping cats comes close to the probability: [[the more boxes, the closer the share is to P_asleep]] (the law of large numbers). The number of sleeping cats still changes from experiment to experiment, by about \u03C3 = \u221A(N\xB7P\xB7(1 \u2212 P)): for 100 boxes and P = 50 % that is about 5 cats. This is how physicists test quantum predictions: they repeat the same experiment thousands of times and count."},today:{title:"Real \u201Ccat states\u201D",text:"Physicists have put larger and larger things into superpositions by shielding them carefully from their surroundings: molecules of 60 carbon atoms (Vienna, 1999), and much larger ones since, pass through a grating of fine slits and interfere like light, and in superconducting circuits a current flows both ways at once. Serge Haroche and David Wineland received the 2012 Nobel Prize in Physics for methods to measure and control single quantum systems, including such \u201CSchr\xF6dinger cat states\u201D of light and of atoms. [[The bigger the object, the harder it is to keep its superposition alive]] \u2014 this is also the main difficulty in building quantum computers."},check:{title:"Check what you learned",text:"Can you tell in advance whether one particular cat will be asleep? (No \u2014 only the chance.) What is the chance after two half-lives? (75 % asleep, 25 % awake.) What does an amplitude of 0.5 mean? (A probability of 0.5\xB2 = 25 %.) Is a real cat ever half asleep and half awake? (No \u2014 opening the box always gives exactly one result, and decoherence does not let a large object stay in a superposition.) Why open many boxes? (Then the share of sleeping cats shows the probability.) Check each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{awake:"Chance that the atom has not decayed, so the cat is awake",asleep:"Chance that the cat is asleep: the two chances add up to 1",state:"Superposition of the atom before it is measured; probability = amplitude\xB2",lifetime:"Mean lifetime of the atom: after \u03C4, 63 % of the cats are asleep",sigma:"Typical spread of the number of sleeping cats in N boxes",all:"Chance that every one of N cats is asleep"},variable:{psi:"State (wave function) of the atom before it is measured",psi0:"State of the undecayed atom",psi1:"State of the decayed atom"},assumption:{thought:"A thought experiment: it has never been done with a real cat, and must not be. The sleeping gas replaces Schr\xF6dinger's poison; the physics is the same.",device:"One atom per box. The counter detects every decay at once and never clicks without one; the gas works at once, and the cat sleeps until the box is opened and never naps on its own.",picture:"The probabilities shown while the box is closed are the quantum prediction for the moment it is opened. A real cat is never in a visible superposition: contact with its surroundings destroys it at once (decoherence). The decayed part is really many states (the particle can fly off in any direction at any moment); here it is counted as one state \u03C8\u2081, and the phases of the amplitudes are left out.",random:"Each atom's decay moment is drawn at random when the box is closed, from the experiment number and the settings, so a run can be repeated exactly. This gives exactly the statistics of quantum mechanics.",fast:"Time runs fast: one second of animation is 10 minutes.",cartoon:"The cat, the counter and the flask are cartoons, not to scale."},param:{closedTime:"Time the box stays closed",halfLife:"Half-life of the atom",seed:"Experiment number",boxes:"Number of boxes",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{closedTime:"The box is opened after this time. The longer it stays closed, the more likely it is that the atom has decayed.",halfLife:"The time in which the atom decays with a chance of 50 %. Real half-lives range from tiny fractions of a second to billions of years.",seed:"Each new run takes the next number, so every run is a new random experiment, and so is every change of the settings. Set a number back to repeat that run exactly.",boxes:"Identical boxes, each with its own cat and atom, all opened at the same moment."},preset:{halfHour:"30 min",hour:"60 min",twoHours:"120 min",short:"10 min",schrodinger:"60 min (Schr\xF6dinger)",long:"120 min",one:"1",ten:"10",hundred:"100"},measure:{openingAsleep:"Chance the cat is asleep when opened",openingAwake:"Chance the cat is awake when opened",elapsed:"Time since the box was closed",asleep:"Cats found asleep",awake:"Cats found awake",halfLives:"Closing time in half-lives",asleepNow:"Asleep if opened now",awakeNow:"Awake if opened now",expectedAsleep:"Expected number of sleeping cats",fractionAsleep:"Share found asleep",amplitudeAwake:"Amplitude of \u201Cawake\u201D now",amplitudeAsleep:"Amplitude of \u201Casleep\u201D now",meanLifetime:"Mean lifetime of the atom",sigma:"Expected spread of the count",allAsleep:"Chance that all cats are asleep"},graph:{chances:"Chances if the box were opened now"},view:{awakeShare:"Awake: {value}",asleepShare:"Asleep: {value}",predicted:"(expected: {value})",amplitude:"\xB7 amplitude {value}",result:"Result",ifOpened:"If the box were opened now",clock:"Closed: {elapsed} / {closed}",openedAfter:"Opened; it was closed for {closed}",atom:"atom",counter:"Geiger counter",gas:"sleeping gas",catAsleep:"Asleep \xB7 z Z",catAwake:"Awake",legendSingle:"Inside: a cat, one radioactive atom, a Geiger counter and a flask of sleeping gas",legendGrid:"Each tile is one box \xB7 open eyes: awake \xB7 shut eyes: asleep (gas)",closedOne:"The box is closed: nobody can know yet whether the cat is asleep. Chance that it will be asleep when opened: {chance}",closedMany:"{n} boxes are closed. Each cat will be asleep with a chance of {chance}",openedMany:"{n} boxes opened: {asleep} cats asleep, {awake} awake (about {expected} asleep were expected)",openedAwake:"Opened: the atom did not decay, so the cat is awake",openedAsleep:"Opened: the atom decayed ({time} after closing), the gas came out and the cat is asleep",barsOption:"Probability bars",amplitudesOption:"Amplitudes"},challenge:{coin:{title:"A fair coin",description:"Set up the box so that, when it is opened, the cat is asleep with a chance of 50 % (\xB1 1 %).",hint:"In Schr\xF6dinger's own example a decay within one hour had a chance of 50 %: keep the box closed for one half-life."},quarter:{title:"One in four",description:"Make the chance that the cat is still awake when the box is opened 25 % (\xB1 1 %).",hint:"Each half-life halves the chance of staying awake: 100 % \u2192 50 % \u2192 25 %."},sure:{title:"Almost certainly asleep",description:"Keep the box closed for at most 60 minutes and make the cat asleep with a chance of at least 95 %.",hint:"Choose an atom with a short half-life: after about 4.3 half-lives only 5 % are left undecayed."},unlikely:{title:"Against the odds",description:"Open a single box in which the cat had a chance of at most 20 % to fall asleep \u2014 and find it asleep.",hint:"Rare things happen if you try often enough. Keep the settings and change the experiment number until it works."},many:{title:"The law of large numbers",description:"Open 100 boxes at once and get a share of sleeping cats within 10 percentage points of the predicted chance.",hint:"With 100 boxes the count usually differs from the prediction by only a few cats. If you are unlucky, try another experiment number."},lifetime:{title:"One mean lifetime",description:"Keep the box closed for one mean lifetime \u03C4 of the atom, so that the chance of a sleeping cat is 63.2 % (\xB1 0.5 %).",hint:"\u03C4 = T\xBD / ln 2 \u2248 1.44 \xB7 T\xBD. For a half-life of 60 minutes that is about 87 minutes."},all:{title:"All ten asleep",description:"With at least 10 boxes, make the chance that every single cat is asleep at least 90 %.",hint:"The chances multiply: P_asleep\xB9\u2070 \u2265 0.9 needs P_asleep \u2265 98.95 %, which takes about 6.6 half-lives."}}}},s={sim:a};export{s as default,a as sim};
